     <article class="story single" data-beat="finance">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">FINANCE</span> · Livemint Money · 2026-10-14 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Diwali 2026: Retail Inflation Hits 5.54% in September, Forecast to Peak at 6.8%</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Diwali 2026</span> <span>#Retail Inflation</span> <span>#CPI</span> <span>#September 2026</span></div>
       <p>India's retail inflation, measured by the Consumer Price Index (CPI), rose to 5.54% in September 2026, up from 4.83% in August 2026, according to the Ministry of Statistics and Programme Implementation (MoSPI). This marks its highest reading under the revised CPI series and the first time it has crossed the 5% mark. As households prepare for Diwali 2026, increased prices across several festive shopping categories could put pressure on budgets.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-14/diwali-2026-retail-inflation-hits-554-in-september-forecast-.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The CPI tracks price changes for a basket of goods and services regularly purchased by households. For instance, if a basket cost ₹1,000 in September 2025, the same basket would cost ₹1,055.40 in September 2026, reflecting a 5.54% increase. However, overall CPI inflation does not mean all products rise at the same rate, with individual categories varying.</p><p>Among items relevant to festive shopping, silver jewellery recorded the highest year-on-year inflation at 87.8% in September 2026, although this was a decrease from 107.11% in August 2026. This means an item costing ₹10,000 in September 2025 would be approximately ₹18,780 in September 2026. Food inflation also climbed significantly to 7.74% from 5.97%, impacting spending on groceries, cooking ingredients, and festive meals, with onions, garlic, and lemons seeing steep increases.</p><p>Inflation also increased across other categories including household utensils, clothing, furniture, appliances, and travel. In contrast, vehicle prices recorded negative inflation of 2.56% in September, indicating a lower price index compared to a year prior. This offers some relief for specific big-ticket purchases.</p><p>Looking ahead, SBI Research’s Ecowrap report projects that retail inflation could peak further, reaching 6.7%-6.8% in November 2026. If these projections hold, households could face additional budget pressure as Diwali approaches, particularly due to the significant impact of food prices on regular spending and seasonal purchases.</p><p>What to watch: Monitor food price trends and overall CPI figures closer to the Diwali festival.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft accurately synthesizes the economic data and projections provided in the source material without adding extraneous information.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
