     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-10-14 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Drifting Buoys Improve Mediterranean Storm Forecasting</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>A new study in the Quarterly Journal of the Royal Meteorological Society demonstrates that drifting buoys equipped with barometers improve forecasts for extreme Mediterranean storms. These buoys provide vital atmospheric data from sea regions where measurements are otherwise scarce, as evidenced by analysis of January 2026's Storm Harry.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-14/drifting-buoys-improve-mediterranean-storm-forecasting.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Coordinated by the M3E initiative, the project integrates real-time pressure and sea surface temperature data into numerical weather models. While Storm Harry caused significant flooding and landslides across the region, researchers note that better in situ data collection is essential for predicting the small, rapidly evolving low-pressure systems that frequently affect North Africa.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Accurate and well-summarized. It correctly identifies the study's scope and the specific storm referenced.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-10-storm-harry-drifting-buoys-early.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
