     <article class="story single" data-beat="positive">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">POSITIVE</span> · The Better India · 2026-10-14 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>IIT Grads' Modulus Housing Delivers 1,500+ Foldable Buildings for Rapid Infrastructure</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Modular Buildings</span> <span>#IIT Madras</span> <span>#Disaster Relief</span> <span>#Healthcare Infrastructure</span></div>
       <p>Five weeks after the Chennai floods, Shreeram Ravichandran, then a second-year civil engineering student at IIT Madras, observed a critical gap in disaster relief: while immediate aid arrived, permanent housing took years. Homes made of thatch had been washed away, leaving families without shelter between initial tents and long-term solutions. This realization, shared with childhood friend and fellow IIT Madras student P Gobinath, became the impetus for Modulus Housing.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-14/iit-grads-modulus-housing-delivers-1500-foldable-buildings-f.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Starting with cardboard and thread experiments in a hostel room, the duo received early support from IIT Madras, including Rs 50,000 from the Centre for Innovation and Rs 2 lakh from Nirmaan. After graduating in 2018, they founded Modulus Housing, initially investing around Rs 20 lakh. Their focus evolved from solely post-disaster buildings to addressing everyday needs for portable cabins, then pivoting during COVID-19 to develop MediCAB – a portable healthcare unit for screening, isolation, and intensive care, deployed in Wayanad, Kerala, in 2020 with Habitat for Humanity.</p><p>Post-pandemic, Modulus expanded significantly, delivering approximately 450 to 470 buildings over 12 to 14 months in challenging terrains like Meghalaya and Nagaland. Their philosophy of making &quot;buildings like products&quot; — manufactured on an assembly line and designed for rapid, low-resource deployment (one truck, no crane, crew of four) — has led to the delivery of over 1,500 modular buildings across 21 Indian states and parts of Africa, serving healthcare, construction, agriculture, and enterprise applications.</p><p>Why it matters: This innovation provides rapid, adaptable infrastructure solutions, making essential services accessible in remote and disaster-affected regions.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft is an excellent summary that captures the origin story, the product evolution, key financial milestones, and the operational philosophy of the company.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://thebetterindia.com/startup/modulus-housing-foldable-buildings-iit-madras-shreeram-gobinath-remote-india-12628490" rel="noopener">The Better India</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
