     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · BBC Science · 2026-10-14 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span><span class="badge quick">quick bite</span></div>
       <h1>Scotland explores crop diversification amid climate change</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Experts are examining how Scotland's farmers might adapt to a changing climate by diversifying crop production. While tea growing remains a niche activity in the region, some believe Scotland's climate could support small-scale production, particularly with the use of glasshouses. </p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-14/scotland-explores-crop-diversification-amid-climate-change.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Campaigners suggest that utilizing abundant renewable energy could make glasshouse farming for Mediterranean vegetables like tomatoes and cucumbers viable. Meanwhile, the James Hutton Institute is using climate modeling to stress-test plants against projected conditions for the next 75 years, which include hotter, drier summers and milder, wetter winters.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief effectively synthesizes the discussion on crop diversification, glasshouse potential, and scientific climate modeling.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
