     <article class="story single" data-beat="national">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · The Hindu · 2026-10-14 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>TMC files complaint over alleged knife attack on Mamata Banerjee</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#national</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>The All India Trinamool Congress (TMC) has filed a police complaint following an alleged knife attack on former West Bengal Chief Minister Mamata Banerjee outside her Kalighat residence on Tuesday. Party leaders claimed the assailant was a civic police volunteer who acted in a violent, intoxicated state; however, officials reported no injuries to the former Chief Minister.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-14/tmc-files-complaint-over-alleged-knife-attack-on-mamata-bane.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Police identified the 38-year-old detainee as Anusua Saha, a civic volunteer posted at West Port Police Station. Authorities stated that the incident involved a heated altercation, and an investigation is underway. While TMC members alleged a larger conspiracy, state officials emphasized that the law will take its course.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief accurately reports the incident details, the party's complaint, and the official police statement, though it slightly generalizes the tone of the altercation.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.thehindu.com/news/cities/kolkata/mamatas-party-files-police-complaint-over-alleged-knife-attack-attempt-on-former-cm-near-her-home/article71580768.ece" rel="noopener">The Hindu</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
